// The system that runs on a machine: the control core, the seam to the driver
// process, and the seam to whatever asks the machine to do something.
//
// The same three cogs as the simulated system, over the same channels, with one
// difference: what stands at the driver's end of the wire. There a cog models
// the plant; here six UDP sockets carry the driver contract across a process
// boundary to `reachy-motord`, which owns the serial port and the bus cycle.
// The core is `motion::MotionCore` in both, stated once, so the two systems
// cannot drift apart in topology. Two more sockets carry the intent edge: a
// script arriving from the process that asked for it, and the session's
// narration leaving for that same process.
//
// Why the driver is a process and not a cog: it must hold a serial port across
// executions and block on multi-millisecond bus transactions. A Rust cog's only
// cross-execution state is schema-resident, so nothing sanctioned can hold the
// port; the online runner has two cog worker threads and no preemption, so
// blocking bus work in an execute body spends half the pool; and an input
// overrun on a slow cog ends the whole process. So the driver is an OS process
// that is no Clockwork process, composed in no box, and this file's business
// with it is the seven sockets it owns of the nine below.
//
// A socket occupies exactly the slot a cog does: it publishes to a channel or
// subscribes to one, and a channel does not say what filled it. So each
// incoming socket is its channel's single publisher here, where the modelled
// driver is in `SimBox`, and the channel graph is untouched.
//
// The datagram on each socket is the schema's bytes and nothing else -- the
// socket layer reads and writes the `message_type`'s Tachyon representation as
// the packet's binary format, at exactly that size. There is no header on this
// seam and no room for one; `driver/wire.clk`'s header belongs to an edge whose
// far end is not a Clockwork socket.
//
// The foot of this file is the composition: the box that closes the loop around
// the core with sockets, the logger box that records the run, and the two
// processes that host them. They are here rather than in the system module next
// door because the grammar admits a `system_target` only in a module that
// generates nothing, and a process needs a module that generates an executable.
//
// The logger box's members are named for what they hold rather than for the
// module their type comes from: a member name shadows a module name in the
// enclosing scope, which the compiler refuses as a redefinition.
// (A comment may not sit directly above a `use`, which is why this one is
// here.)

#![generate(cpp, cpp_exe)]
#![cpp(namespace=brenn_reachy::robot)]

use [cpp, proto, proto_conv] cogs::config;
use cogs::motion;
use cogs::script as script_vocab;
use cogs::session_cmd;
use driver::goal;
use driver::health;
use driver::pose;
use motion::timeline;
use @clockwork::clockwork::logging::log_writer_config;
use [cpp, proto, proto_conv] @clockwork::clockwork::logging::writers::channel_message_rates_config;
use @clockwork::clockwork::logging::writers::logger;
use [] @clockwork::clockwork::logging::writers::logger_channels;
use [cpp, proto, proto_conv] @clockwork::clockwork::logging::writers::logger_config;
use @clockwork::clockwork::logging::writers::logger_status;

// The driver's sample stream, arriving. Bound on the loopback: the driver sends
// each cycle's sample here, and this socket is `DriverPose`'s one publisher in
// this composition.
//
// The loopback bind is the whole of what these five incoming sockets trust.
// Nothing off the machine can reach them and a datagram of the wrong size kills
// this process, but a datagram of the right size from any other process on the
// unit is indistinguishable from the driver's: machine state, fault evidence and
// a torque-off confirmation can all be fabricated by a local sender, and the
// driver's dead-man does not answer it, because that fail-safe fires on silence
// and a sender's whole effect is to prevent silence. The same holds in reverse
// for the two ports `reachy-motord` binds. Accepted for now -- every process on
// this unit is ours -- and what would end it is a peer check the seam has no
// room for yet: TODO(motord-seam-trust-boundary)
udp_socket PoseIn
{
    address: 127.0.0.1;
    port: 7402;
    direction: incoming;
    message_type: Tachyon<pose::PoseSample>;
}

// The edges the driver reported: a de-torquing it latched, a confirmation it
// read back, a bus it declared gone.
udp_socket EvtIn
{
    address: 127.0.0.1;
    port: 7403;
    direction: incoming;
    message_type: Tachyon<health::DriverEvent>;
}

// How one out-of-band transaction turned out.
udp_socket AuxOutIn
{
    address: 127.0.0.1;
    port: 7404;
    direction: incoming;
    message_type: Tachyon<health::AuxOutcome>;
}

// What the driver's rotating read of the status registers saw, one report per
// servo visited.
udp_socket HealthIn
{
    address: 127.0.0.1;
    port: 7405;
    direction: incoming;
    message_type: Tachyon<health::HealthReport>;
}

// Everything the driver knows about its own run, republished on a cadence: the
// release it wrote at process start, the rows that would not read back, when it
// first published a sample and first heard from the host, and every counter it
// keeps. Its own port for the reason each of the others has one -- the arrival
// port is the discriminator on this seam.
udp_socket StatusIn
{
    address: 127.0.0.1;
    port: 7407;
    direction: incoming;
    message_type: Tachyon<health::DriverStatus>;
}

// The goal stream, leaving. `port` on an outgoing socket is the destination --
// the compiler admits `remote_port` only on a bidirectional socket, and the
// emitter hands this number to the send -- so 7401 is the port
// `reachy-motord` binds, not one this process holds.
udp_socket GoalOut
{
    address: 127.0.0.1;
    port: 7401;
    direction: outgoing;
    message_type: Tachyon<goal::GoalSetpoint>;
}

// What the session asks of the driver: keep the dead-man fed, take torque off
// now, or run this transaction. Its own destination port, because one datagram
// type per port is what tells the driver's reader what it is holding -- with no
// header on this seam, the arrival port is the discriminator.
udp_socket SessionOut
{
    address: 127.0.0.1;
    port: 7406;
    direction: outgoing;
    message_type: Tachyon<session_cmd::SessionCmd>;
}

// The intent edge, arriving. A script the voice host compiled from what
// somebody asked for -- in the harness, `reachy-ask`'s one gesture -- and this
// socket is `ScriptsIn`'s one publisher in this composition.
//
// The sender is on this machine and built from this tree, which is the whole of
// what makes a fixed-size reader safe here: a datagram of the wrong size kills
// this process, and the process's death is a minimum-risk-condition event. Off
// this machine nothing can reach the port at all. The rest of the seam's trust
// story is `PoseIn`'s, unchanged: TODO(motord-seam-trust-boundary)
//
// Everything a sender could get wrong about an intent -- an undecodable body, a
// foreign pod, a redelivery, a schedule that will not fit -- is refused outside
// this process, at the edge that received it. What crosses this port is already
// a `Script`.
udp_socket ScriptsIn
{
    address: 127.0.0.1;
    port: 7409;
    direction: incoming;
    message_type: Tachyon<script_vocab::Script>;
}

// The session's narration, leaving. Cumulative: every publish is the whole
// story so far, so a reader that started late or restarted is healed by the
// next one, and this socket needs no retention of its own.
//
// `port` on an outgoing socket is the destination, as `GoalOut`'s is, so 7410
// is the port the voice host binds and not one this process holds.
udp_socket ReportsOut
{
    address: 127.0.0.1;
    port: 7410;
    direction: outgoing;
    message_type: Tachyon<timeline::Timeline>;
}

// The control loop as it runs on a machine: the core three cogs and nine
// sockets -- seven in place of the modelled driver, two carrying the intent
// edge.
//
// The topology is `SimBox`'s with two publishers replaced. Where that box puts
// a cog at the driver's end and reads its scripts out of an input log, this one
// puts sockets to processes at both ends: the driver's, and the one that asks
// the machine for something. Nothing the core does distinguishes them: a cog
// reads channels, and a channel does not say what filled it.
//
// Every channel has exactly one publisher here too. The core's own self-loop --
// the decision tick reading back the goal channel it publishes -- adds a
// subscriber and no publisher, and the outgoing sockets on `DriverCmd` and
// `ReportsOut` are subscribers beside the cogs that read them.
box RobotBox
{
    new core: motion::MotionCore;

    // The intent edge, both directions. A script arrives already compiled and
    // already screened, and the session's narration leaves for the process that
    // sent it: the sender is what tells an operator whether the machine took
    // the request, and it reads that off this stream.
    new scripts_in: ScriptsIn;
    connect scripts_in to motion::ScriptsIn;

    new reports_out: ReportsOut;
    connect motion::ReportsOut to reports_out;

    // The sample stream, arriving from the driver process. The core's two
    // control-rate cogs run on it and the session reads it for one thing only:
    // whether the driver is still publishing at all.
    new pose_in: PoseIn;
    connect pose_in to motion::DriverPose;

    // The goal stream, leaving for the driver's gate. The tick's own read-back
    // of this channel is the core's, drawn there.
    new goal_out: GoalOut;
    connect motion::DriverCmd to goal_out;

    // What the session asks of the driver, and what the driver answers.
    new session_out: SessionOut;
    connect motion::SessionCmdChan to session_out;

    new aux_out_in: AuxOutIn;
    connect aux_out_in to motion::DriverAuxOut;

    // What the driver says about itself: an edge it reported and a servo's
    // error bits are the two kinds of fault evidence the decision tick cannot
    // see.
    new evt_in: EvtIn;
    connect evt_in to motion::DriverEvt;

    new health_in: HealthIn;
    connect health_in to motion::DriverHealth;

    // What the driver says about its whole run. Logged only: nothing in this
    // system reads it, and it exists so that a log written by a logger which
    // attached whenever it attached still holds every fact the run is verified
    // from.
    new status_in: StatusIn;
    connect status_in to motion::DriverStatus;
}

// What writes the run down: the framework's two writer cogs, in a process of
// their own.
//
// The wall-clock runner does no logging itself -- its output-log plumbing
// belongs to the deterministic path -- so an online run is recorded by a logger
// process reading the logged channels out of the control process's shared
// memory. Which channels is not decided here: it is the `LogWriterConfig` the
// compiler generates from the `ChannelLoggingPolicy` set next door, and this box
// binds that generated file.
//
// Logging is observation only. No channel flows out of this box into the control
// loop, so a logger that died, or whose writes fail, costs the run its records
// and nothing else -- logging never gates motion and never gates de-torquing.
//
// The whole box is one `cpu_domain` away from being the production shape
// instead: the same two cogs on a second domain -- the dev host -- with the
// built-in TCP bridge carrying the channels, which puts the log on real disk
// with no step that pulls it off a unit. TODO(online-host-logger)
box RobotLogger
{
    new init_cog: logger::LoggerInitCog;
    new status_publisher_cog: logger::LoggerStatusPublisherCog;

    // The writer's arena: its buffer pool plus what it keeps about the channels
    // and schemas it is writing. The pool is one second of the configured write
    // rate plus the 8 MiB schema reserve, in 256 KiB buffers, so 4 MiB/s asks
    // for 12 MiB of it; the rest is headroom for the per-channel bookkeeping.
    // Not the framework example's 300 MB, which is sized for a vehicle's worth
    // of video and lidar.
    new memory: HeapMemory(max_size=33'554'432);

    // Which channels are written, and everything the writer needs to write them
    // without asking anybody: slot counts, message sizes and each schema's own
    // definition. Generated from the logging policies next door, so the set is
    // stated once and in the same vocabulary the simulated system logs.
    new logged_channels: SerializedDataFile(
        representation=Tachyon<log_writer_config::LogWriterConfig<>>,
        path="cogs/system_robot.motion_robot.RobotCpu_event_logger_config.tachyon");
    connect logged_channels to init_cog.log_writer_config;

    // Where the records go and where the channels are read from.
    new logger_params: SerializedDataFile(
        representation=Protobuf<logger_config::LoggerConfig>,
        path="cogs/robot_logger.textproto");
    connect logger_params to init_cog.logger_config;

    // What counts as a channel gone quiet, for the status message only.
    new rate_floors: SerializedDataFile(
        representation=Protobuf<channel_message_rates_config::ChannelMessageRatesConfig>,
        path="cogs/robot_logger_rates.textproto");
    connect rate_floors to init_cog.channel_rates_config;

    // The writer itself, built on the first execution and kept from then on. It
    // holds a file descriptor and an io_uring across executions, which is why it
    // is an extern type in a state slot rather than anything schema-resident.
    new state: State(
        representation=logger::LoggerCogState,
        memory_resource=memory,
        init=init_cog.logger_state);
    connect state to status_publisher_cog.logger_state;

    // What the logger says about itself: the configuration it came up with, its
    // state, and the rate it is seeing on each channel. Logged like anything
    // else, so a run's records say whether the thing writing them was healthy.
    connect init_cog.logger_config_out to logger_channels::LoggerConfigChannel;
    connect status_publisher_cog.logger_status to logger_channels::LoggerStatusChannel;
    connect status_publisher_cog.channel_rates to logger_channels::LoggerMessageRatesChannel;
}

// The machine the control loop runs on: the unit's own CPU.
//
// The launcher config this domain renders names the two cog processes; the
// driver, the voice host and the audio device are three more OS processes on the
// same machine and are merged in from the hand-written app configs the
// `simplelaunch_src` lines name, so one launcher starts and stops all five. The
// renderer emits only a process-description path as a generated app's args, so
// every process here runs on the compiled-in pinion defaults -- see
// `robot_logger.textproto`, which restates them.
//
// A motion run starts a twin of this config instead, with the host and the pod
// left out: the harness's intent source binds `ReportsOut`'s destination port
// and sends to `ScriptsIn` for the duration of a run, and the host does both
// too, while a motion run must need no audio hardware at all. The twin is
// `robotcpu_harness.textproto` in this package's BUILD file, which says so at
// the rule; its content merges the same rendered sources, and only its rule is
// hand-maintained, because the build updater knows of one config here.
cpu_domain RobotCpu
{
    simplelaunch_src: "//driver:motord_launch.textproto";
    simplelaunch_src: "//host:host_launch.textproto";
    simplelaunch_src: "//pod:pod_launch.textproto";
}

// Two processes on one machine over one executable, wall-clock: the control
// loop, and the logger that watches it. `robot_clk_exe` is the executable the
// compiler synthesizes for this file under `cpp_exe`, named for the file stem
// and therefore the name `Process(executable=...)` spells; each process is
// started from it with its own process description, which is what makes one
// binary two processes.
//
// It links `online_main` rather than the offline main: the same `main`, whose
// `run_deterministic` is a refusing stub -- the right failure mode for a binary
// composed over live sockets, where a deterministic run would have nothing to
// read.
//
// Separate processes and not separate boxes in one, because the logger reads
// channel buffers out of shared memory while a cog worker thread is publishing
// into them, and a writer stalled on a filesystem must not block the two threads
// that command a machine.
box System
{
    new box: RobotBox;

    new proc: Process(executable=robot_clk_exe);

    apply HostProcess(process=proc) in box;
    apply HostCpuDomain(cpu_domain=RobotCpu) to proc;

    new logger_box: RobotLogger;

    new logger_proc: Process(executable=robot_clk_exe);

    apply HostProcess(process=logger_proc) in logger_box;
    apply HostCpuDomain(cpu_domain=RobotCpu) to logger_proc;
}
